#edb/ref 17592186059364 :question/id #uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" :question/math-academy-id "e-138" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059365 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe7d686-fceb-5893-8824-fa874f02fc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{x^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056108} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f9463-ac37-5073-9bd0-1c581c32bc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "36x^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056109} {:answer/id #uuid "04d9595d-edc4-5313-ac43-3dca55608c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "12x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056110} {:answer/id #uuid "10cd59df-624f-539a-877b-a604f947553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{x^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056111} {:answer/id #uuid "249afd6c-aac5-5c78-b111-d1096dacdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{x^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3900d9b-b037-5571-8ce9-f4acb088f7bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056107}] :question/id #uuid "af71cdb8-8b5c-5075-89d8-624e1e63c073" :question/math-academy-id "q-10737" :question/problem "If $y = \\frac{6}{x^{6}}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88cdd62d-36a3-56b4-ad53-5ef4ae1b675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "36x^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056282} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dfa5e4-a09b-590b-9c48-2a9d810b3a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "36x^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056283} {:answer/id #uuid "f534d307-ad17-5cc0-a5e0-fb7193d9c6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "36x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056284} {:answer/id #uuid "20aee839-3ad1-51cc-a0bc-0bfaa6c0cf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "42x^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056285} {:answer/id #uuid "49e38165-fae8-5b78-8583-4b479e2a62ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e325c6f6-b672-5f86-8889-03d865df916b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056281}] :question/id #uuid "6d51dc01-44b0-56ff-973b-78a9457915f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-1214" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^{6}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a944189-6813-5164-879c-eab76a5beda0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "12x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058070} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc3a755-7484-5e4e-9aa0-2019d5c8e3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "12x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058071} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc4c54-58ba-50c8-bfe6-6e8e10018dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "24x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058072} {:answer/id #uuid "6b798069-ae3e-55a5-aef7-730100f68aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "7x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058073} {:answer/id #uuid "76130876-a748-5eaa-b66a-f6da481d85b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{4x^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186058070} :answer-field/id #uuid "be302e32-8941-5ade-84be-3b85b8b0d362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "4a354b54-aabb-5908-97e9-b4409b8eca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-272" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 4x^{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060875 :tutorial/content "The **constant multiple rule** states that if a function $f(x)$ is multiplied by a *constant* factor $k$, then in order to differentiate $kf(x)$ we can take the derivative of $f(x)$ and then multiply by $k$ afterwards:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(k f(x)\\right) = k \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(f(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, to differentiate $y=6x^2$, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= 12x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "882aa12a-1be9-5fa0-8b2f-2a42007270f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 345 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sin xdx &= -\\cos x + C \\\\\n\\int\\sec^{2} xdx &= \\tan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sec x\\tan xdx &= \\sec x + C \\\\\n\\int\\csc x\\cot xdx &= -\\csc x + C \\\\\n\\int\\csc^{2} xdx &= -\\cot x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "bfa0c70d-6cf7-5c69-8995-d9f6c980a4ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087387 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087388 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087538 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087539 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13a4f93-e1f9-5884-a39c-c79a4bcb3e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2}y = x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084460} {:answer/id #uuid "c3444d68-c1e5-5947-a906-9f7391cdd707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{e^{x}}{x}y = x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084461} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4eab8-6e07-5ae7-96d3-a9034a47b11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4xy = \\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084462} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0bf6cc-cd01-5352-adcb-7f989d4f9813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2} = \\frac{1}{1 - y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084463} {:answer/id #uuid "511a9ab3-97fa-5de3-a077-f939d6fb35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - (\\tan x)y = \\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084463} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c68cfab-095b-58cc-9da7-ba0916b77f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103560" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE has the form $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$, with coefficients depending only on $x$. The equation $y^{\\prime}-x^2=\\frac1{1-y}$ contains nonlinear dependence on $y$ through the reciprocal $1/(1-y)$, so it is not linear. The other equations have only first powers of $y$ and $y^{\\prime}$ with coefficients depending on $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084850} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084851} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084852} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084853} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04c8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085012} {:answer/id #uuid "da5a2b38-cb05-5079-8a67-44b3023a6a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085013} {:answer/id #uuid "011ca4e6-3122-58c6-a064-72bf61a7cc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{2}) = x^{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085014} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc23e01-156d-58f7-838b-e84160a81d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y}{x}) = \\frac{\\cos x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085015} {:answer/id #uuid "6436279c-38d0-5c1d-a595-b7ab9c80521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186085016} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c0749d-dee0-5f31-b150-55643e10de7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17540" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos x$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here $P(x)=1$, so an integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{\\int1\\,dx}=e^x$. Multiplying the equation by $e^x$ gives $e^xy^{\\prime}+e^xy=e^x\\cos x$. By the product rule, the left side is $(ye^x)^{\\prime}$, so $\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^x)=e^x\\cos x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c34b9817-7572-5e31-83bd-d3b526daebf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} + 2xy = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087103} {:answer/id #uuid "dcea8c9a-81e6-5544-8145-205e168f69d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 2xy = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087104} {:answer/id #uuid "f322b6fc-44a0-55f7-915b-d14169f9dc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy^{2} = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087105} {:answer/id #uuid "2828fc07-5e90-5f6d-b4a7-fc668fb5f126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 6x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087106} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1cbc52-aff7-517a-9bd1-88ad12396227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2x = 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087106} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee99775-1c6a-5ae6-afcf-8bb35a100894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8787ecbb-bcde-5e1b-a5ed-9d23815522e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087109} {:answer/id #uuid "b54535fc-a8dc-5ada-b428-b490dbf1a18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087110} {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} {:answer/id #uuid "6b67e556-e64d-523c-9321-f7fb1846ebc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9de150-76e4-5439-86c8-31f5b6d852a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos e^{\\arctan (x^{2} + \\ln x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7759e328-b997-576f-b938-3880d1aeb387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} {:answer/id #uuid "c28a08c9-ed5f-57c9-a678-ebf32e926c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087116} {:answer/id #uuid "f09d6fcb-7508-53dc-9ebf-082a30a5867b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087117} {:answer/id #uuid "1046320f-9c93-59e8-ae7e-8dbe81821495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{1}{x}y + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087118} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc89d23-a930-5ede-b341-6ac4428f6277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = 2x - x\\frac{dy}{dx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b999b3-f12d-5d5e-aa21-cfda0ea51796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11d5a1d-e93d-5a5d-8111-e33d997e2841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087211} {:answer/id #uuid "e572ebc9-141f-578b-93ec-080a0deeb913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot 2x) = 2x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087212} {:answer/id #uuid "fd667700-3e8d-53d0-9c71-264753a1f8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{2}) = x^{2} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087213} {:answer/id #uuid "6e285ba0-0b5f-5e5a-a7bd-e5b3de551a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{2}}) = e^{x^{2}} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087214} {:answer/id #uuid "76e91a95-c637-5848-86e4-e944f3eee17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087214} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8fe340-5770-5791-befc-e802db4415ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "8973fb88-5098-5e4b-a0b0-25638565dd93" :question/math-academy-id "q-8546" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 2xe^{-x^{2}}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here $P(x)=2x$, so $I(x)=e^{\\int2x\\,dx}=e^{x^2}$. Multiplying through and using the product rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^2})=e^{x^2}\\cdot2xe^{-x^2}=2x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087806} :step/expected-seconds 240.0 :step/id #uuid "29f2610f-f30f-5b70-bb79-098ffb891c67" :step/math-academy-id 593012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088747}}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088805 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.**\n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x)$. The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3,\\qquad x\\ne0\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2$, we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot x^{2}) &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot x^{2})dx &= \\int 3x^{2}dx \\\\\ny \\cdot x^{2} &= x^{3} + C \\\\\ny &= x+\\frac{C}{x^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot x^{2}) &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) \\cdot x^{2} + y \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) &= 3x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + y \\cdot 2x &= 3x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy &= 3x^{2}\\checkmark \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14550 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088806 :tutorial/content "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)$\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n$I(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,$.\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)$:\n$I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)$\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)$\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)$\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y$:\n$$yI(x)=\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,dx+C,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{I(x)}\\left(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,dx+C\\right).$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14551 :tutorial/title "The Integrating Factor Method"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088808 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x$. In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n$y' - xy = 0$.\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x$. Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n- Now, consider the equation\n$y' - xy = 6$.\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$. Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n$y' + xy = 2y$.\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n$y^{\\prime}+xy-2y=0$, or $y^{\\prime}+(x-2)y=0$.\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2)$. Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." :tutorial/id #uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14653 :tutorial/title "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(x)$, $b(x)$, and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with\n\n$$\na(x) = x,\\quad b(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation\n\n$$\nx(y')^2 + e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y)$, and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088892 :tutorial/content "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}y^{\\prime } + \\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y &= \\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\\ny^{\\prime } + \\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y &= \\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis division is valid on any interval where $a(x)\\ne0$ at every point. The weaker condition that $a$ is not identically zero does not permit division at its zeros.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)}\n$$\n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x\n$$\n\non an interval where $\\sin x\\ne0$, the standard form is found by dividing through by $\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime } + y(\\frac{e^{x}}{\\sin x}) &= \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\\\\ny^{\\prime } + ye^{x}\\csc x &= \\cot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4056 :tutorial/title "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096323 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5$, calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} = 5g^4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 5g^{4} \\cdot (12x^{3} + 3x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(3x^{4} + x^{3})^{4}(12x^{3} + 3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096324 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable.\n\nLet $u=1-x^2$. Then\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2x\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096333 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0$.\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x\\right) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\ln 3 \\right) \\\\ &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0 \\\\ &= 3 e^x. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096334 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\\ &= 4 e^x+ 3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\\ &= 4 e^x+ 12x^{3} - 2x. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096335 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln 2 \\\\ &= 3 \\ln 2 \\cdot 2^{x}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096336 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)' \\\\ & = 5 (\\ln x)' \\\\ &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{5}{x}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096337 :question/id #uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" :question/math-academy-id "e-3628" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\ln x+x^2-1$, find $f'(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)' \\\\ &=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0 \\\\ &=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096338 :question/id #uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" :question/math-academy-id "e-3629" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e$. By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right) \\\\ &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right) \\\\ &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096339 :question/id #uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3630" :question/problem "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1$." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nat that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = 1} &= \\left.(\\frac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\frac{1}{x})\\right|_{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln 4} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" :question/math-academy-id "e-528" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-530" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041209/assets/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-531" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cdeff7-1bd1-52cc-b4d2-58765a1e715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y=-\\frac1{2(x-1)}+\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094041} {:answer/id #uuid "e264582e-6aa3-5da8-b05d-657aca25a556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094042} {:answer/id #uuid "43d2def0-e7d1-54fb-834f-84b6876ea10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094043} {:answer/id #uuid "518e0850-647a-5199-8686-13cfd746ca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094044} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc7592f-e41a-5c1f-9369-d6bc76a6ea98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186094043} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd411f0-cb37-5900-866e-8862a9b0a1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "d93ad997-2d1a-586a-bd5a-d470614a6ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-119078" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(x - 1)^{2}y^{\\prime } =-2$ that satisfies the condition $y(2) = 1$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne1$, write $y'=-2(x-1)^{-2}$. Integration gives $y=2/(x-1)+C$. Apply $y(2)=1$:\n$$1=2+C,\\qquad C=-1.$$\nThus $y=2/(x-1)-1$, on the interval $x>1$ containing the initial point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f651d57-fd13-5223-b101-19044f5a3b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094635} {:answer/id #uuid "0c189129-a43b-5083-8426-a8d988b5bcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2} - C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094636} {:answer/id #uuid "441b0a08-b772-5959-b466-9ce911e9e6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094637} {:answer/id #uuid "63128db0-6242-55ce-a386-75928939d9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094638} {:answer/id #uuid "7833225a-e7d7-58d8-a243-47f76b32d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = 11x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186094637} :answer-field/id #uuid "702c2b7a-1035-57b4-98fc-9ee1ec455ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "e1c8faa6-71a3-52b0-9c4c-3e41ed01dd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-14245" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f^{\\prime }(x) + 11x = 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Rearrange to $f'(x)=-11x$ and integrate:\n$$f(x)=\\int-11x\\,dx=-\\frac{11}{2}x^2+C.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85752216-72d1-52c5-a307-1f7593bb2baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095378} {:answer/id #uuid "bffd2b2f-d555-5e17-8756-8a754febf526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095379} {:answer/id #uuid "eef892e3-6947-592f-9345-311b595e0536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095380} {:answer/id #uuid "05327ffd-b783-558f-8116-edad45453171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095381} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa9f7-a4b9-5ff4-84f6-37fdf189f3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095378} :answer-field/id #uuid "a72fa786-3004-51a5-a233-17a9a216dfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48625" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^4+3x$. Then $f=u^2$ and $u'=4x^3+3$. The chain rule gives\n$$f'=2u u'=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9acd2481-0ec3-5994-9168-964cb3c474c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{x} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095618} {:answer/id #uuid "b3631b1e-e008-5fb9-a825-84c8ecba10b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = e^{x} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095619} {:answer/id #uuid "c508b657-4c25-56c5-960c-217135f76315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}e^{x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095620} {:answer/id #uuid "f83fba29-574c-5d24-b1a6-4adbe9283fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095621} {:answer/id #uuid "38b83252-dae4-5abd-841f-1818a0da90fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095622} :answer-field/id #uuid "62dcfac4-1b46-5254-bc04-921bedd65cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "36f54028-9879-56b6-bec9-c97cd80fb58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49942" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0) =-1$.\n\n$2f^{\\prime }(x) + e^{x} = 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The equation gives $f'=-e^x/2$, so $f=-e^x/2+C$. At $x=0$,\n$$-1=-\\frac12+C,\\qquad C=-\\frac12.$$\nTherefore $f(x)=-\\frac12(e^x+1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991a4d75-b082-5ddb-9e0e-daec82c04821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-2e^{2x} - x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095672} {:answer/id #uuid "b018b6ae-1940-5995-bcd3-0cb355cdfdbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-4e^{2x} - 2x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095673} {:answer/id #uuid "c2357d5d-526c-504c-b3d2-09cc85042828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-e^{2x} - x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095674} {:answer/id #uuid "eed6b0a7-f89a-5f99-8fa6-663cdc54a4e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-e^{2x} - 2x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095675} {:answer/id #uuid "5a53ae9e-07c4-5848-85e2-a1c6b214ea93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-2e^{2x} - 2x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095672} :answer-field/id #uuid "144fd343-32aa-51c0-ba1c-2d78f454b0d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "cb82aa2f-3e4a-523a-94c2-c95aa3cb8ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-50136" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{2x}=-2x.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89aee0c2-41c4-50d9-95e7-d07999f8e43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = 5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095678} {:answer/id #uuid "a8264bab-88eb-5c1b-8cf6-4b5658e50c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = Cx^{2} + 10x + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095679} {:answer/id #uuid "c8066636-9a63-5ade-85aa-07f7e0be5cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095680} {:answer/id #uuid "0a099fae-b25d-5abb-a50a-c5dc1eda7e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095681} {:answer/id #uuid "1a40f9c2-33a9-5233-bec8-c51acd272979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095680} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c860608-75a8-5451-a1a7-a0683cdbc84f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715" :question/math-academy-id "q-50139" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d53-2adc-5935-a97d-5a586599fa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095684} {:answer/id #uuid "f4387418-5075-52c7-b685-924f2791a4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095685} {:answer/id #uuid "035eddbc-75fb-53ba-bc61-c5e0c4f3f025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095686} {:answer/id #uuid "12887777-c8a6-5e16-bf30-ed290559c1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095687} {:answer/id #uuid "7a68fbfe-8f1c-512a-8cc2-1b00fe18af49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045453} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095686} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5a4615-20d2-563a-8738-3f3905aa1549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "3e04bc45-f34f-56fc-9a0e-8643597c4852" :question/math-academy-id "q-50148" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853aeea8-65f0-5fb9-8258-80667aea2abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50195-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095690} {:answer/id #uuid "a94dc35e-7f42-58d5-bacb-d6ebe1a5ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50195-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095691} {:answer/id #uuid "efb18e51-9c8d-5683-8dc6-f9dd9a0e7082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50195-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095692} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9b1208-989e-52fe-9319-003ca0fe7916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50195-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095693} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f4a30-dc1d-5668-a728-9b3bc043b41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50195-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095691} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa5bcf1-a2e1-5611-80a8-e28d46985c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "a33d5262-3afa-580b-b512-aba7c2576e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-50195" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2(x-1)?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041209/assets/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87248665-cb5c-5039-a61b-f40377bba96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50196-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095696} {:answer/id #uuid "ca53b3f7-7fae-554a-ae0b-ae63a12f5e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50196-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095697} {:answer/id #uuid "e65a9044-67d3-586d-be0f-d72ddf67972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50196-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095698} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdad7c2-d1f6-5540-909f-d9988ae726bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50196-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095699} {:answer/id #uuid "5e919e55-cc7c-5065-9aea-29667e6de4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045452} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1061/Source/Images/q-50196-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186095699} :answer-field/id #uuid "71784ece-a52d-5f27-a140-7ccee85a3e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465} :question/id #uuid "27b042e7-c453-5f49-bac9-de5985bbacde" :question/math-academy-id "q-50196" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097720 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(a^x\\right) = a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of $e^x$.\n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n\n$$\na^x = e^{\\ln (a^x)}\n$$\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n$$\na^x = e^{x\\ln a}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x\\ln a}) &= e^{x\\ln a} \\cdot \\ln a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(a^{x}) &= e^{x\\ln a} \\cdot \\ln a \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln a^{x}} \\cdot \\ln a \\\\\n&= a^{x}\\ln a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "551a7005-80c0-5cee-b28e-f3b565aaf250" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13257 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Second Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097727 :tutorial/content "The exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nhas the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same.\n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." :tutorial/id #uuid "a71b1a8d-93f4-5e48-ab3a-bd7220d50f18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2611 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097728 :tutorial/content "If we replace the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "061a5c54-700a-5793-b266-cf156584544a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2612 :tutorial/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097729 :tutorial/content "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\n.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{x + h} - e^{x}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{x} \\cdot e^{h} - e^{x}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(e^{x} \\cdot (e^{h} - 1))/(h) \\\\\n&= e^{x} \\cdot lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{h} - 1}{h}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b006e01-04f6-5f6b-b435-b0f89163d7a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2613 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097730 :tutorial/content "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1f46211-3766-53d0-bcc7-30bb9056b948" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2615 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097731 :tutorial/content "For a general logarithm of base $a$, the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(\\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x = \\log_{10} x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\log_{10} x) = \\frac{1}{x\\ln 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log_{a} x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln a} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln a} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x\\ln a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a7450e4-ec0e-5a5e-9c86-9430eb889be7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2616 :tutorial/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function with a General Base"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\frac{df}{dx} = \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dg}(g^{3}) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(1 + 2x) \\\\\n&= 3g^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 6g^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nfor $g$, we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 6(1 + 2x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097846 :tutorial/content "Often, differential equations aren't easy to solve. Some are so difficult that they can only be solved using entire rooms full of very powerful computers!\n\nHowever, there is one type that's often quite easy. These are differential equations that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = g(x)\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose that we want to find the solution to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this differential equation by integrating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nFor the left-hand side, calculating the indefinite integral of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\ngives $y+C_1$ because differentiation is reversed by integration. For the right-hand side, this is a reasonably standard integral that we know how to compute. So, we get\n\n$$\ny + C_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 1+2x \\mid + C_2\n$$\n\nWe now tidy up the result, solving for $y$ and combining the two constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a third constant $C=C_2-C_1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+C_1&=\\frac12\\ln|1+2x|+C_2\\\\\ny&=\\frac12\\ln|1+2x|+(C_2-C_1)\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\ln|1+2x|+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution we obtained is called a **general solution** because it involves the arbitrary constant $C$. This is in contrast to \"particular\" solutions, in which the constant is set to a fixed value. We will learn more about particular solutions later, but for now we will focus on general solutions.\n\n**Note:** Remember that we combined the constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a single constant $C$. For these types of differential equations, we can usually do this, and for that reason, we often go straight to adding a constant $C$ to one side of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "a136fcee-de1f-5f62-b419-01f7fc6b3279" :tutorial/math-academy-id 952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097847 :tutorial/content "Often, instead of the general solution that involves an arbitrary constant, we want a **particular solution**. A particular solution is a solution that satisfies the differential equation *and* some other condition on the function.\n\nAs a simple example, consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = 2x\n$$\n\nWe can find the general solution by integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int 2xdx \\\\\ny &= x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn its current form, the function $y$ represents infinitely many solutions, each with a different value of the arbitrary constant $C$.\n\nHowever, suppose we want to find a **particular solution** that satisfies the condition $y(3)=10$. To do this, we can solve for the appropriate value of the arbitrary constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + C \\\\\n10 &= 3^{2} + C \\\\\n10 &= 9 + C \\\\\n1 &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired particular solution is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3298ec-d6a5-5792-9ebc-90f1b44de891" :tutorial/math-academy-id 957 :tutorial/title "Particular Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101942 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x}$, the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y$, into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2e^{2x}}_{y'}=2\\underbrace{e^{2x}}_y=2e^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b27482c3-602c-5761-9cd5-bec55b2c2932" :tutorial/math-academy-id 947 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166583 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166584 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e2483ea-aaa8-5ec9-8ceb-1c77c24a3768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165823} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef5d8d-d533-58b7-8279-515b42a4dcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165824} {:answer/id #uuid "18cb3c7b-429e-50a7-9e5f-a98f4442103b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165825} {:answer/id #uuid "58305345-1cb5-54c3-b58e-fc70576c839d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165826} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac54382-1709-57fc-ac99-bbc84710cd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186165826} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bab884-1ca9-54e8-a6ce-407833d8ab94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8fcc14-3b71-5da6-b5dd-7babd909bb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166171} {:answer/id #uuid "cc48ca6b-4aa3-5db8-bea8-d0c431202662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} {:answer/id #uuid "38f746bf-71f6-5862-b734-46ca998885c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166173} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5b041-244e-5910-a46e-1d2d8b63ba6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166174} {:answer/id #uuid "61ba28ba-32e1-59ce-87b7-bf847008593c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045454} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a53d5f-04a9-5740-b2e4-3c2ac8d9a527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045450} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\nII. $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\nIII. $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168001 :tutorial/content "An **ordinary differential equation** is an equation that involves a function with one variable (usually denoted as $y$), its derivatives, and an independent variable (usually denoted as $x$).\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}-3xy^2 = y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis an ordinary differential equation (ODE).\n\n**Watch out!** Here, the function $y=y(x)$ actually depends on the independent variable $x$.\n\nThe **order of an ordinary differential equation** is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nIn our example above, the highest derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}\n$$\n\nwhich has order $2$. Therefore, the order of the ODE is $2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d012836-922f-5da1-92d2-1a48b741d1ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5014 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168015 :tutorial/content "A first-order ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be **autonomous** if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\ny'=f(y)\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -5y^2\n$$\n\nis autonomous, while the equation\n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{y+2}{x^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* autonomous since the expression for $y'$ on the right-hand side contains the independent variable $x$ explicitly, outside the function $y=y(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0803a18-b9e8-5c2e-90a8-6f8946cd5d0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5169 :tutorial/title "Autonomous Ordinary Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168016 :tutorial/content "A first-order **initial value problem** (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}y(e)=-2}\n$$\n\nis a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}\n$$\n\ntogether with the initial condition\n\n$$\n{\\color{red} y(e)=-2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46723f7e-094f-5e9c-af08-e9e7c0b4a88e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5170 :tutorial/title "First-Order Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233947 :topic/id #uuid "67b4251f-708a-567e-be2f-62ab6bc1a315" :topic/math-academy-id 7005 :topic/title "Interpreting Additive Opposites and Addition Using Absolute Value"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233948 :topic/id #uuid "ba836877-f89f-5358-82cf-e5aae236cdcd" :topic/math-academy-id 7007 :topic/title "Solving Problems Using the Four Operations on Rational Numbers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233949 :topic/id #uuid "24a28f9b-1f4f-5349-9353-6e0a556259c4" :topic/math-academy-id 7011 :topic/title "Rational Numbers as Finite or Repeating Decimals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233950 :topic/id #uuid "1f20d05d-0574-5a52-95b8-8c5c229c66b2" :topic/math-academy-id 7220 :topic/title "Using Experimental Probability to Make Predictions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233951 :topic/id #uuid "d775b4ca-f41c-55f3-8888-bc0b6b2311c8" :topic/math-academy-id 7221 :topic/title "The Likelihood of an Event"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233952 :topic/id #uuid "7df3b87a-2fb3-57cb-9c88-d7cef86d721a" :topic/math-academy-id 7222 :topic/title "Using Probability to Make Predictions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236040 :policy/accuracy-update-rate 0.2 :policy/base-half-life-days 1.0 :policy/early-practice-discount-power 1.0 :policy/future-horizon-days 7.0 :policy/gate-slow-implicit true :policy/id #uuid "f6218cf2-e157-5240-93c6-faafa321cfcd" :policy/initial-accuracy 0.8 :policy/initial-retention 1.0 :policy/interval-growth 2.0 :policy/maximum-failure-multiplier 4.0 :policy/maximum-half-life-days 36500.0 :policy/maximum-speed 4.0 :policy/minimum-speed 0.25 :policy/overdue-failure-slope 1.0 :policy/question-selection-weights [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240980 :question-weights/activity-type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045447} :question-weights/initial-easy 60.0 :question-weights/initial-hard 10.0 :question-weights/initial-moderate 30.0 :question-weights/remedial-easy 45.0 :question-weights/remedial-hard 0.0 :question-weights/remedial-moderate 55.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240981 :question-weights/activity-type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045448} :question-weights/initial-easy 40.0 :question-weights/initial-hard 20.0 :question-weights/initial-moderate 40.0 :question-weights/remedial-easy 90.0 :question-weights/remedial-hard 0.0 :question-weights/remedial-moderate 10.0}] :policy/retention-update-order {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462} :policy/review-threshold 0.5 :policy/speed-exponent 2.0}]}
